{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB13679 - MONDO_7448",
  "verbalization": "Dexchlorpheniramine, a histamine H1 receptor antagonist, is linked to Familial Dermatographia through a series of indirect pathways. The evidence suggests that Dexchlorpheniramine may influence the development of Familial Dermatographia via its role in modulating allergic reactions, as it is associated with Urticaria, a condition that is also linked to Familial Dermatographia. Urticaria, in turn, is indicated by three different drugs—Tripelennamine, Phenyltoloxamine, and Antazoline—each of which is associated with Familial Dermatographia. This suggests a potential mechanism where Dexchlorpheniramine, through its effect on histamine, may contribute to the pathophysiology of dermatographia by influencing the immune response or skin reaction to stimuli. The shared indication of Urticaria across these drugs implies a common pathway in their action, potentially involving the regulation of inflammatory responses. While the exact mechanism remains unclear, the evidence points to a possible link between Dexchlorpheniramine and Familial Dermatographia through its role in modulating allergic reactions, supported by the overlapping indications across multiple drugs.",
